<item>
            <title>[IGNITE-11604] Drop column does not remove column from internal schema</title>
                <link>https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/IGNITE-11604</link>
                <project id="12315922" key="IGNITE">Ignite</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Discovered this during the work on &lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/IGNITE-11541&quot; title=&quot;Dynamic columns and indexes can be lost after the cluster restart&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;IGNITE-11541&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;IGNITE-11541&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (see &lt;tt&gt;StaticCacheDdlTest.testDropColumn&lt;/tt&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a quick debug I see the following: in &lt;tt&gt;GridQueryProcessor#onSchemaFinishDiscovery&lt;/tt&gt; we call &lt;tt&gt;DynamicCacheDescriptor.schemaChangeFinish(msg)&lt;/tt&gt;, which eventually calls &lt;tt&gt;QuerySchema.finish(op)&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;br/&gt;
Inside that method we have the following: the &lt;tt&gt;message.columns()&lt;/tt&gt; collection has the field name in upper case (in the test it&apos;s &quot;FIELD_TO_DROP&quot;), but the entity&apos;s fields map contains lower-case names (&quot;field_to_drop&quot;). As a result, we do not remove the field from the query entity, this query entity is saved to the stored cache data and field re-appears after restart.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
                <environment></environment>
        <key id="13223281">IGNITE-11604</key>
            <summary>Drop column does not remove column from internal schema</summary>
